@page "/exboard"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Board

<DocsPage Product="ExBoard" Title="Overview"
          Lead="A Board is JSON an AI composes once and a workflow runs again and again without AI: grids, pivots and charts laid out in rows over typed Board Inputs, each input checked against its data's shape before anything is drawn.">

    <DocsSection Title="Composed once, run without AI">
        <p>
            The data a workflow obtains is not known until it runs: which columns, which types, a grid, a pivot or a
            chart. A Board says it in JSON. Its <code>inputs</code> name the data and declare each column's type, written
            while whoever composed it knew what the data means; its <code>views</code> are grids, pivots and charts over
            those inputs; its <code>layout</code> is rows of views, each its share of the row, never free placement. The
            Board is saved, and each run binds every input to that run's data. ExBoard holds no workflow: it reads the
            Board, checks its inputs, and draws it.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>BoardSpec.Read</code> reads a Board, and refuses what cannot be drawn with one sentence naming the path
            and the reason, as a Chart Spec does: a version other than 1, a member a Board does not have, a view naming
            a column its input does not declare, a decimal column with no format, a layout that leaves a view out or
            places one twice. A grid view is <a href="exgrid/columns">a grid built at run time</a>, a pivot view holds a
            Pivot Layout, and a chart view is a Chart Spec whose series name inputs in their <code>data</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The <code>ExBoard</code> component draws a read Board with each input bound to a Snapshot. Binding checks the
            Snapshot's shape against the input's declaration first; a column missing or of another type draws nothing but
            the refusal, naming the input and the column, so a change upstream is caught on the run that meets it. Grids
            and pivots stretch to their share of the row; a chart is drawn at its share of the Board's <code>Width</code>.
            Change the Board below and draw again: remove the grid's <code>format</code>, have the pivot place a field
            the input does not declare, or make the chart's series an <code>amountPerPeriod</code>, which a day's
            P&amp;L with no interval cannot be drawn as.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(DeskBoardBuiltin)" Mud="typeof(DeskBoardMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Board/DeskBoard.cs"])" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Links narrow by values">
        <p>
            A link names a grid or a pivot, the views it narrows and the columns it compares:
            <code>{"from": "blotter", "to": ["byTrader", "daily"], "on": ["desk"]}</code>. Select rows in the grid, in any
            column, and each view the link reaches shows only the rows whose values in those columns are the selected
            rows' values. In the Board above, select an FX trade: the pivot shows FX's traders and the chart FX's days.
            A selection of several rows, with Ctrl or Shift, lets through every one of their values.
        </p>
        <p>
            It narrows by values, not by rows, so it reaches views over other inputs that declare the same columns, and
            keeps its meaning when the data moves on. On two columns it lets through the selected rows' own pairs:
            rows (Rates, Jan) and (Credit, Feb) let through no (Rates, Feb). Text is compared as a grid's value list
            compares it, without regard to case. A narrowed grid keeps the sort and the filter set on it; a pivot is
            computed again over the rows let through, and a chart drawn from them. When the selection is dropped,
            because the grid was sorted or filtered or the selection cleared, the narrowing goes with it: a view is
            never left narrowed by a selection that is no longer there.
        </p>
        <p>
            A link from a pivot narrows by the cells selected in its report. A cell stands for the rows under it: a
            trader's cell for that trader's trades, a subtotal for every Item below it, a grand total for every row the
            pivot shows. An Item the pivot hides is under no cell. In the Board above, the pivot links to the chart on
            <code>trader</code>: select Chen's cell and the chart shows Chen's day alone. A pivot's link compares only
            fields placed in its rows or columns, since those are what a cell names. When another link narrows the
            pivot, its report is computed again, its selection is dropped, and what its cells narrowed is let go. A
            selection that reaches rows the report has not drawn yet narrows nothing, and the Board says so under
            the pivot rather than narrowing by part of it.
        </p>
        <p>
            Reading refuses a link that starts at a chart, compares a field a pivot does not place in its rows or
            columns, narrows its own view, names a view or a column that is not there, compares a column of one type with one of another, or
            would close a loop: links run one way.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Outputs raised on a confirm">
        <p>
            What the user chooses is handed on to the workflow's next step only when they say so. An output names a
            grid, the columns whose values it hands on and the label of the action that raises it:
            <code>"outputs": {"review": {"from": "blotter", "on": ["day", "trader"], "confirm": "Send to review"}}</code>.
            The action is drawn under the grid; a selection on the way raises nothing, and a press with nothing
            selected says so. Select rows in the Board above and press <em>Send to review</em>: <code>OnOutput</code> is raised
            with a <code>BoardOutput</code>, shown here as its JSON.
        </p>
        <p>
            An output is a value condition, the selected rows' values in its columns, with the version of the input's
            data they were taken against. It hands on values, not rows: every row holding the chosen values is taken,
            so an output meant to name single rows names a column that tells them apart, such as a trade's id. The next
            step runs it where the data lives, or takes the rows with <code>BoardOutput.Take</code>, which refuses data
            of another version, and other data at version 0, which names none: the values the user chose are taken
            from the data they chose them in, or nothing is.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="What the user changes, kept as a Board Diff">
        <p>
            Running a Board never rewrites it. The user may still sort and filter a grid, drag its columns' widths
            and order, pin or hide a column, rearrange a pivot, hide a chart's series, narrow its range or switch its
            axis. Each change raises <code>OnDiff</code> with the whole <code>BoardDiff</code>: one part for each view
            that differs from what the Board writes, a grid's View State and the columns hidden from it, a pivot's
            layout, a chart's series, range and axis. A selection, and the narrowing a link makes of it, is not a
            change to the Board and raises nothing. Sort the blotter above to see its JSON.
        </p>
        <p>
            The host keeps <code>ToJson()</code> for its user and hands it back next time: it reads it with
            <code>BoardDiff.Read</code> and passes it as <code>Diff</code>, laid against the Board as it is then. A part that no longer fits, a
            view the Board no longer has, a column its grid no longer shows, a filter on a column its input now declares
            another kind, is dropped by name and the rest applies;
            the Board says what it dropped above its views. A column the Board has added since is shown, not taken for
            one the user hid. Press <em>Draw with my changes</em>, or first remove the blotter's P&amp;L column from the
            JSON, to see both. Making the user's view the default is writing a new Board.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Still to come">
        <p>
            ExBoard is not yet published as a package.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

